ng is actually missing. On idempotent re-runs over an already-migrated repo, all checkpoints will be in the `existing != nil && !force` path and almost all sessions will have artifacts present, so we pay full v1 transcript I/O and decompression for every session of every checkpoint just to discover nothing needs repair. This is O(checkpoints × sessions × transcript bytes) wasted work.\n**Suggested change:** Invert the order. First walk `v2Summary.Sessions` calling `hasFullSessionArtifacts`, collect the set of session indices that need backfill. If empty, return early without touching v1 at all (skip both `ReadCommitted` and `collectV1SessionsForPacking`). Only when there's at least one missing session, read the v1 summary and lazily `ReadSessionContent` for the specific sessions needed (and the corresponding session-id lookup if the index map fails). This cuts the common re-run path from N transcript reads to zero.\n\n### Finding 2: `migratedFullCheckpoints` accumulates full transcript bytes across the entire migration\n**Severity:** high\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:175` (`var migratedFullCheckpoints []migratedFullCheckpoint`) through `packMigratedFullGenerations` at end\n**Issue:** `migratedFullSession.content` is a `*checkpoint.SessionContent` whose `Transcript []byte` field holds the raw transcript bytes (verified at `cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/checkpoint.go:424-433`). The migration loop appends every migrated checkpoint's sessions (each carrying a full transcript) into the `migratedFullCheckpoints` slice and only partitions/streams them into git after the loop completes. For a repo with many large transcripts (multi-MB each is plausible), peak heap is roughly `Σ transcript_bytes` across the whole v1 list. This can easily reach multiple GB on large repos and is unbounded by `migrateMaxCheckpointsPerGeneration`.\n**Suggested change:** Stream packing. Maintain a small running buffer sized to `migrateMaxCheckpointsPerGeneration` (default 100). When the 