* low\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/checkpoint/v2_generation.go:145`; consumer `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/generation_repair.go:repairOneV2GenerationMetadata`\n**Issue:** `AddGenerationJSONToTree` calls `UpdateSubtree` with `MergeKeepExisting`, which always re-marshals the blob, re-merges entries, and calls `storeTree`. Because git is content-addressed, the resulting tree hash *will* equal the input when entries are identical, so the `if newTreeHash == treeHash` check in `repairOneV2GenerationMetadata` does correctly skip the commit/ref update. But each call still pays one blob marshal + one tree marshal + a tree-object lookup per archived generation, even when nothing changes. The earlier `generationMetadataEqual(current, gen)` guard prevents this in most cases. Acceptable as written; the secondary check is defensive and cheap relative to the timestamp computation that precedes it.\n**Suggested change:** None required. Optionally add a doc comment on `AddGenerationJSONToTree` noting that callers should compare the new vs existing `GenerationMetadata` first to avoid the rebuild, since the function does not internally short-circuit.\n\n### Finding 5: `repairPushTarget` correctly resolves remote URL once\n**Severity:** none (verification)\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/strategy/generation_repair.go:120-131`\n**Issue:** Verified — `repairPushTarget` is called once before the candidate loop, returns early with `\"\"` if no candidate has `RemoteOID`, and the resolved URL is passed to each `repairOneV2GenerationMetadata` call. `remote.PushURL` is invoked at most once per migration. No fix needed.\n\n### Finding 6: `RepairV2GenerationMetadata` always runs at end of migration\n**Severity:** low\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:95` in `runMigrateCheckpointsV2`\n**Issue:** Called unconditionally even when zero checkpoints were migrated. It walks `listArchivedV2GenerationCandidates` and computes raw transcript timestamps for each — O(archived generations × checkpoints in each). Acceptable because: (a) it's only invoked from the explicit `entire migrate` command, not a hot path; (b) the outer command is already an opt-in one-time-ish operation; (c) repair is idempotent and a no-op when `generationMetadataEqual` is true. The cost compounds with Finding 3's heavier per-generation timestamp scan, but only on this single command.\n**Suggested change:** Optionally short-circuit when `result.migrated == 0 && !force` and the candidate list is empty. Not required.\n\n### Finding 7: `collectV1SessionsForPacking` reads all v1 sessions even when only one is missing\n**Severity:** medium (subsumed by Finding 1, but call out explicitly)\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:collectV1SessionsForPacking`\n**Issue:** Even on first-time migration where the dual lookup (by session-id and by index) is needed, the function eagerly loads every session's `SessionContent` up front. Given that the packing path could process sessions one at a time, eager pre-load is unnecessary memory pressure that compounds with Finding 2.\n**Suggested change:** Convert into a lazy resolver: a closure or small struct that exposes `Lookup(sessionID string, idx int) (*SessionContent, error)` and reads from v1 on demand, caching only what was already requested. This naturally pairs with the inversion proposed in Finding 1.\n\n### Finding 8: `nextMigratedGenerationNumber` is fine as-is\n**Severity:** none\n**Location:** `cmd/entire/cli/migrate.go:nextMigratedGenerationNumber`\n**Issue:** O(archived) once per migration. No concern.\n\n---\n\nSummary: the two real issues are **(a)** the eager v1 transcript read in the idempotent re-run path (Finding 1) and **(b)** the unbounded in-memory accumulation of `SessionContent.Transcript` across the whole migration (Finding 2). Both should be fixed before this lands on repos with non-trivial checkpoint counts. Finding 3 (cleanup-path slowdown) is medium and easy to mitigate by preferring the cheap timestamp path with raw-transcript